"The pome was sweet\nNever wanted to quit\nMight've thought so\nBut never acted on it\n\nHad a million seeds within\nEach akin to some thought\nSome were in the plates of others\nSome were left inside to rot\n\nPome wanted to recite and sow\nFrom poems to seeds it had\nHad no column not a row\nTo right and hence the skin got bad\n\nPome was sweet red\nSeeds were stiff white\nSome had the juicy coverings\nSome rotted and lost their might\n\nIt was sure just red pome\nNot the granite deep in the crust\nPome might look stiff outside\nIt's nowhere near the rocky rust\n\nIt tended to become like granite\nPome tried, pome failed\nIt mended itself day and night\nAnd finally it realized\n\nThe rotten seeds are futile\nNot every seed should go in plates\nNot every pome gets an eater\nAnd it's okay to not to elucidate\n\nPome is now different\nTend not to worry about rotten seeds\nPome accepts the partial sweetness\nIt now trim or just pass by weeds"